VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want this video to be that initial guide that I wish we had when we first got FDM and photopolymer printers. Only now we still have years of experience with these machines that shape our expectations. Those who have experience with 3D printing, remember the'

[02:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'past. How much have we learned about workflow features , such as using anti-aliasing and image blurring? We are back to the beginning of learning again. But for this video, to gently get into the subject, I chose familiar tests and models that we'

[02:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually want to see for comparison, not just pretty display samples that HeyGears picked up and which, well, don\'t quite match what real users want to type. Let\'s start with the FDM version of the multi-colored dragon, which became'

[02:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'popular when the tooling change came along. And here is the same model printed on the G1X, now fully textured with bright, vibrant colors. That\'s the difference. Now, if you want to do something like this, first you'

[03:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'need a color file. This means you need to texture an empty STL file elsewhere before importing it into Blueprint. I mainly used GLB and OBJ files, but other formats are supported . And I used Meshary with'

[03:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hints or reference images because I was testing the printer, not my ability to learn digital painting in a very short time. Plus, I had Meshary credits left over from when I did their commercial segment, and I had no other reason to'

[03:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'use them. But yes, a significant portion of this color, not all of it, but a significant portion, is generated by artificial intelligence. So, of course, it\'s not always stable. In fact, it is never stable. But it allows me to color'

[03:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'familiar models very quickly. HeyGears does intend to add a coloring feature directly to Blueprint , but this will also be implemented using AI. In their case, it would be Trypophobia. I hope I didn\'t make a mistake in the pronunciation. Although, however you'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'achieve this, the texture you provide, i.e. the colored shell, it is just as important as the model itself. You can paint it manually digitally if you want, and there are many tutorials showing how to apply textures to models in various'

[04:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'programs, but right now the models need to be painted separately from the slicer. To set up printing in Blueprint, create a project, select G1X, platform, application profile, which offers different settings depending on the desired'

[04:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'result, and then import your model, which should be pre- painted. All this is familiar, but it is in the matter of orientation that we need to break old habits. When working with polymers or FDM, we keep important'

[04:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'surfaces away from the support attachment points. Here, however, the soluble support surrounds the model and creates a kind of "bed" for it. According to my tests, surfaces facing down into the support material generally came out'

[04:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sharper than those facing up. It\'s also important to understand how the technology works here, because we\'re firing microscopic droplets from a head, which is essentially an inkjet printer, and curing them with UV light after'

[04:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'they\'ve landed on a flat surface. This is different from extruding plastic or illuminating an LCD image on a layer of polymer. Where these droplets land, how they spread and coalesce, affects the surface. So'

[05:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the habits, settings, and results of working with polymers simply don\'t apply here. The Blueprint controls the placement of supports, but you can\'t make a cavity here. We don\'t extrude liquid polymer and light up an entire layer. Here, the projected'

[05:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ink will not hold up as a small protrusion, as the UV light instantly cures it. It will simply fall into the void or flow over the edge. Next is support material. We dissolve it in water. I'

[05:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'used a heated ultrasonic bath, not that ridiculous washing station that HeyGears sells, and I think HeyGears should release their own ultrasonic cleaner, why not? You would still buy it because it'

[05:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'says HeyGears on it. Right now, it seems like heat is doing a lot more than vibration here. I leave the parts in it at 40–50 °C overnight, then run an ultrasonic cycle for about 30 minutes , before'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'scrubbing everything with a toothbrush and leaving to dry. It\'s not fast, but it doesn\'t require manual labor, alcohol, or methylated spirits. In addition, HeyGears said they are working on a more soluble formula. It is important to remember that'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'this dirty water cannot be poured down the drain. You should follow the disposal instructions of your local waste service or just do as I do: pour into a clear container and place outside.'

[06:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Because these layers cure during printing, you can remove the model from the build platform without gloves. However, in the HeyGears video they are working with gloves on, so I still wear them. Maybe this is just a habit from working'

[06:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with resin printers, but again, check the description for this device. I\'ll update the information as soon as I find out, but for now I\'ll work with gloves on. So, let\'s explore the details. The XP Finder test I did in the previous video'

[06:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'was done on my prototype, and this new one was printed on their machines, and it came out sharper. I wanted to compare this to an FDM printer with a 0.2mm nozzle, but it misses thin'

[06:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'lines and struts, and some holes are filled in due to rendering features in the slicer. I also used a HeyGears RS30 printer, which clearly reproduces almost all of this geometry. And this'

[07:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the difference between resin and FDM printing. As for the G1X, it actually comes surprisingly close to a resin print in terms of detail, but there are softer edges throughout. And the HD cube test from J3D'

[07:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'confirms this. Those tiny notches and intricate shapes are present, even the inscriptions. So this is real physical detailed geometry, not just details created with color.'

[07:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But detail is not dimensional accuracy, so I also used J3D calibration cubes , and they are detailed, they just don\'t fit together the way they should after a resin printer. So, this is'

[07:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'not at all the same as a calibrated resin printer. Now for those with experience in resin printing, hopefully it\'s obvious that the V3 calibration cones are not a valid test here, as the solvent support covers the entire model,'

[07:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'so it\'s obvious that all the cones printed, even the ones that normally don\'t come out. I chose this simply because I wanted to show the familiar geometry and the sword-through-the-skull test. But an attempt to break off the sword resulted in'

[08:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the model flying into pieces. This is a very dense and hard resin . I wouldn\'t call it fragile like the kind some people use—it crumbles as soon as you look at it. I would say that these models don\'t have much flexibility,'

[08:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but it takes a lot of effort to break something. Actually, all of these models were sent from China, and the only one that broke was the heavy hammer on the back of this caricature of mine that I made. And this is another example of a'

[08:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cartoon render that came out in this color in Meshmixer, but I then used it to print in color on FDM so I could compare. But yes, the destruction of the cones during the test effectively ended my detail testing, but it\'s not a failure. These are just'

[08:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tests for resin printers using new technology. So, I\'m showing them here to demonstrate: this device is more suitable for figurines and demonstration models, not for parts that need to be assembled or precisely fitted. I also'

[08:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'ran some anti-aliasing tests, and again these are just your usual benchmarks. There are no anti-aliasing settings here, because such a function simply does not exist. And of course, the orientation in which I printed them on the'

[09:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'platform is different. So I\'m showing the surfaces again so you can compare them to other technologies that you might be familiar with. With the supports, I also asked to print a round sphere, which showed some flattening of the layers—'

[09:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'probably due to the fluidity of the material or the difference in shapes, or because the support material mixed with the base material during curing. Again , I\'m still trying to figure this out myself. But HeyGears'

